Josephus calls John a good man and attributes fear of rebellion to Herod: Antiquities 18.116–119 (https://lexundria.com/go?q=J.+AJ+18.+116-119&v=wst).

The wording of Mark 1:11 is NIV. The repeated personal wording following it is a spoken paraphrase. Mark 1:12 is KJV. The chapter text was checked against the user's Mark 1 notes (https://github.com/kmorrow7210-stack/mark-1-912/blob/main/Mark%201.md).

The David reference is offered as a thematic echo, not a demonstrated quotation by Mark. See 1 Samuel 16:13 (https://www.biblegateway.com/verse/en/1%20Samuel%2016%3A13). The explicit statement that the Spirit remained upon Jesus occurs in John 1:32–33.

Forms of σχίζω connect Mark 1:10 (https://biblehub.com/text/mark/1-10.htm) and Mark 15:38 (https://biblehub.com/text/mark/15-38.htm).

Mark uses εὐθύς at 1:10 and 1:12. The narrative directly joins baptism, affirmation, and wilderness. In 1:12 (https://biblehub.com/text/mark/1-12.htm), τὸ πνεῦμα is the subject and αὐτόν the object. The context establishes the same Spirit; the article alone does not carry the whole argument. Jesus is acted upon in this clause, but ἐκβάλλει is an active verb, not a passive construction.

Compare the leading language of Matthew 4:1 (https://biblehub.com/text/matthew/4-1.htm) and Luke 4:1 (https://biblehub.com/text/luke/4-1.htm). Mark's stronger verb does not establish Jesus' reluctance or physical coercion.

"Descends/sends" is an English preaching device. The Greek verbs do not rhyme. Commissioning is an interpretation of the narrative sequence. Mark does not quote Isaiah 61:1 here.

Mark 1:13 (https://biblehub.com/text/mark/1-13.htm) says the angels were ministering to Jesus. It does not say they arrived only after the forty days, accompanied Him into the wilderness, or set a literal table. The draft says He was cared for there without inventing those details.

The first move stops at the baptism so the second can unfold the driving. Jesus' preaching after John's imprisonment, in Mark 1:14, is reserved for later rather than anticipating the next scene here.

